<h3 data-start=3D"8586" data-end=3D"8642" class=3D"">5.3. M=C3=A9thodes de =
gestion du cycle de vie dans Spring</h3>
<p data-start=3D"8644" data-end=3D"8791" class=3D"">Spring offre aussi des =
interfaces comme <strong data-start=3D"8684" data-end=3D"8704">Initializing=
Bean</strong> et <strong data-start=3D"8708" data-end=3D"8726">DisposableBe=
an</strong> pour personnaliser l'initialisation et la destruction des beans=
.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"8793" data-end=3D"8796" class=3D"">
<h2 data-start=3D"8798" data-end=3D"8832" class=3D"">6. Gestion des Scopes =
des Beans</h2>
<h3 data-start=3D"8834" data-end=3D"8900" class=3D"">6.1. Scopes des Beans =
: Singleton, Prototype, Request, Session</h3>
<p data-start=3D"8902" data-end=3D"9039" class=3D"">Spring supporte plusieu=
rs types de scopes pour les beans, qui d=C3=A9terminent la dur=C3=A9e de vi=
e et la port=C3=A9e des objets cr=C3=A9=C3=A9s par le conteneur.</p>
<ul data-start=3D"9041" data-end=3D"9321">
<li data-start=3D"9041" data-end=3D"9110" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"9043" data-end=3D"9110" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"9=
043" data-end=3D"9056">Singleton</strong> : Un seul bean partag=C3=A9 =C3=
=A0 travers toute l=E2=80=99application.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"9111" data-end=3D"9184" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"9113" data-end=3D"9184" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"9=
113" data-end=3D"9126">Prototype</strong> : Un nouveau bean est cr=C3=A9=C3=
=A9 chaque fois qu=E2=80=99il est demand=C3=A9.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"9185" data-end=3D"9265" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"9187" data-end=3D"9265" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"9=
187" data-end=3D"9198">Request</strong> : Le bean est associ=C3=A9 =C3=A0 u=
ne requ=C3=AAte HTTP dans une application web.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"9266" data-end=3D"9321" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"9268" data-end=3D"9321" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"9=
268" data-end=3D"9279">Session</strong> : Le bean est associ=C3=A9 =C3=A0 u=
ne session HTTP.</p>
</li>
</ul>
